module gunbc.roadmap_validation_oracle


data validation_oracle_authority_note: String = "The single authority for WHAT a verification run is run AGAINST, kept separate from whether that run passed. Verify used to be hardcoded pending, so a finished agent left an uncommitted diff and a human ran the receipts by hand; running them automatically is only worth anything if the thing being run cannot be edited by the agent whose work it judges. That is what this module models: the oracle a declared validation names, its observed identity, and the pin captured before the agent started. Per-oracle identity is std.content_hash.Fnv1a64Structural — content_hash_tagged_structural and content_hash_atom, the same fnv1a64 structural authority v1's surface fingerprints and v2's node hashing already use — and the ordered set fold combines those structurals into one ContentHash for the pin seam; cryptographic families are unwritable on OracleFound.identity (review 45483). Pin expected-versus-observed is extdeps.pin via admit_pin_integrity: this module supplies the SUBJECT and the OBSERVATION and borrows the adjudication."

data validation_oracle_grain_note: String = "WHY THE PINNED IDENTITY IS THE ENTRY FILE AND NOT THE NAMED DECLARATION ALONE, recorded because the narrower grain is the intuitive choice and it is UNSOUND for this purpose. A declared validation is `gunbc run --claim-run --entry E --function F`. Pinning only F's declaration text would leave every helper, data row, and fixture in E editable by the agent whose work F judges — and a witness is overwhelmingly assertions ABOUT values declared beside it, so weakening a fixture weakens the oracle just as effectively as weakening the assertion. The digest is therefore over the whole entry file. Going FURTHER — pinning the entry's whole import closure — is equally wrong in the opposite direction: the subject under test is IN that closure (a frontier witness imports the frontier it tests), so a closure pin would refuse the very diff the node exists to produce. The entry file is the boundary that separates oracle from subject, and it is not an arbitrary one: the corpus already enforces it structurally, because a `test fn` outside a `*_test.dag` file is a naming-hygiene violation at the floor. So the pin's grain rides an existing wall rather than inventing a line. THE RESIDUE THIS GRAIN LEAVES IS NOT DEFERRED — it is made unreachable, and validation_oracle_unpinned_dependency_note owns that decision, its measurement, and its lane. An earlier version of this note ended here with a dissolve-on and shipped the hole; review 45278 was right that a fail-open in the feature's own safety property cannot be a declared scaffold, and the correction is recorded there rather than re-spelled here."

data validation_oracle_declaration_scan_note: String = "The declaration count is a LINE-START scan over the entry source, and its limits are stated rather than discovered. A top-level .dag declaration is unindented, so requiring the line to BEGIN with a declaration keyword followed by `<name>(` or `<name><` excludes the common false positive — the same identifier appearing inside one of this corpus's large note strings — without pretending to be a parse. Both keywords are enumerated because a claim entry point is overwhelmingly `test fn` while its helpers are `fn`, and either may be the function a contract names; matching only the bare keyword reported every witness entry as a missing oracle, which is how this was found — the paired green/red controls went red together, which a scanner that recognised nothing would also produce, and that is precisely why the non-vacuity control sits beside them. It is used for exactly one decision, how MANY declarations answer to the contract's name, because that count is what separates three genuinely different states with three different remedies: none is a missing oracle, one is the oracle, and more than one means the contract does not determine which declaration the claim runner will pick. That last state is not hypothetical here — this substrate accepts a duplicate top-level declaration silently and fn names are not module-scoped — so the arm has a real producer rather than being coverage by illusion. dissolve-on: feature:cited-symbol-resolution — the same trigger extdeps.pin already names; when identifiers resolve against the containment tree, this scan is replaced by that resolution and the ambiguity arm is answered structurally instead of textually."

type ValidationOracleRef {
  entry: std.types.FilePath
  function: std.types.NonEmptyStr
}

data validation_oracle_observation_note: String = "Three arms because three remedies. OracleFound carries the identity that the pin is adjudicated against. OracleMissing means the entry source was obtained and the contract's function is NOT declared in it, or the source could not be obtained at all; the host's own reason travels in `detail` rather than being reclassified here. Those two causes share an arm deliberately and the sharing is bounded: neither can green, both stop the line, and the located detail says which one happened. What is NOT claimed is that this arm distinguishes a deleted file from an unreadable one — the Filesystem.Read surface reports success plus a message and exposes no typed absence, so inventing that distinction from an error string would be a classification this module cannot decide. dissolve-on: feature:filesystem-read-typed-absence — the read surface carries absent as its own arm, and OracleMissing splits along it. OracleAmbiguous is the count>1 state above."

data validation_oracle_unpinned_dependency_note: String = "review 45278, and the reviewer was right that a declared residue is not good enough HERE. The entry-file pin closes the direct attack — an agent editing the witness that judges it — but a witness may import assertions and fixtures from OTHER test modules, and those are oracle, not subject. dag/test/claim/scm_compatibility_shape_witness_test.dag is the measured specimen: it imports four test.claim modules, so weakening test.claim.scm_compatibility_git_witness would green Verify without moving the pinned digest. That is the same fail-open the pin exists to close, reached one import away, and shipping it behind a dissolve-on would have been exactly the unmarked-scaffold move DESIGN section 5 forbids — a green that means nothing for 125 of the corpus's 928 witness files (measured, not estimated).\n\nSo the residue is made UNREACHABLE rather than deferred: an oracle whose entry imports a test-side module REFUSES, typed and counted, instead of verifying against a partially pinned oracle. This is deliberately the cheaper of the two remedies the review named. The complete fix — pinning the whole oracle-side dependency partition — needs a transitive import closure, and computing one here would mint a SECOND import resolver beside the containment tree the namespace lane is making the single naming authority (DESIGN section 3). Refusing needs only the module NAME, which the import line already states, so it forks nothing.\n\nWhat this does NOT catch, stated because the boundary is real: a test helper that is not named as a test module — a plain fn file under a non-test module path — is structurally indistinguishable from a subject module here, so it neither refuses nor is pinned. Closing that requires the same partition as the complete fix.\n\nLANE, because a trigger with no owner is a park and the review was right to say so: this dissolves on v2.compiler.source_authority's derived path-module rows (the module identity vs storage thread in DESIGN's open threads), which is what makes an entry's closure computable without a second resolver. When those rows land, the oracle-side partition is pinned in full and these refusals become verifications."

type OracleObservation
  = OracleFound {
      oracle: ValidationOracleRef,
      identity: std.content_hash.Fnv1a64Structural,
    }
  | OracleMissing {
      oracle: ValidationOracleRef,
      detail: String,
    }
  | OracleAmbiguous {
      oracle: ValidationOracleRef,
      declaration_count: Int,
    }
  | OracleDependsOnUnpinnedOracle {
      oracle: ValidationOracleRef,
      imports: List<String>,
    }

data validation_oracle_test_module_prefixes: List<String> = ["test.", "v2.test."]

fn validation_oracle_import_target(line: String) -> String {
  if !starts_with(s: line, prefix: "import ") {
    ""
  } else {
    let rest = substring(s: line, start: 7, end: string_length(s: line))
    match split(s: rest, delimiter: " ") |> first {
      Present { value: head } => head
      Absent => ""
    }
  }
}

fn validation_oracle_import_is_oracle_side(module_path: String) -> Bool {
  any(
    validation_oracle_test_module_prefixes,
    prefix => starts_with(s: module_path, prefix: prefix),
  )
}

fn validation_oracle_unpinned_dependencies(source: String) -> List<String> {
  split(s: source, delimiter: "\n")
    |> map(line => validation_oracle_import_target(line: line))
    |> filter(m => validation_oracle_import_is_oracle_side(module_path: m))
}

data validation_oracle_identity_tag: std.types.NonEmptyStr = "gunbc.validation-oracle/v1"

data validation_oracle_set_tag: std.types.NonEmptyStr = "gunbc.validation-oracle-set/v1"

fn validation_oracle_source_identity(source: String) -> std.content_hash.Fnv1a64Structural {
  content_hash_tagged_structural(
    tag: validation_oracle_identity_tag,
    payload: content_hash_atom(value: source as std.types.NonEmptyStr),
  )
}

data validation_oracle_declaration_keywords: List<String> = ["fn ", "test fn "]

fn validation_oracle_declaration_line_matches(
  line: String,
  function: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
) -> Bool {
  any(
    validation_oracle_declaration_keywords,
    keyword => starts_with(
      s: line,
      prefix: join([keyword, function as String, "("], ""),
    ) || starts_with(
      s: line,
      prefix: join([keyword, function as String, "<"], ""),
    ),
  )
}

fn validation_oracle_declaration_count(
  source: String,
  function: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
) -> Int {
  count(filter(
    xs: split(s: source, delimiter: "\n"),
    predicate: line => validation_oracle_declaration_line_matches(
      line: line,
      function: function,
    ),
  ))
}

fn validation_oracle_observe_from_source(
  oracle: ValidationOracleRef,
  source_readable: Bool,
  source: String,
  read_error: String,
) -> OracleObservation {
  if !source_readable {
    OracleMissing {
      oracle: oracle,
      detail: join([
        "validation oracle source could not be obtained at ",
        oracle.entry as String,
        ": ",
        read_error,
      ], ""),
    }
  } else {
    let declarations = validation_oracle_declaration_count(
      source: source,
      function: oracle.function,
    )
    if declarations == 0 {
      OracleMissing {
        oracle: oracle,
        detail: join([
          "validation oracle source at ",
          oracle.entry as String,
          " declares no ",
          oracle.function as String,
        ], ""),
      }
    } else if declarations > 1 {
      OracleAmbiguous {
        oracle: oracle,
        declaration_count: declarations,
      }
    } else {
      let unpinned = validation_oracle_unpinned_dependencies(source: source)
      if count(unpinned) > 0 {
        OracleDependsOnUnpinnedOracle {
          oracle: oracle,
          imports: unpinned,
        }
      } else {
        OracleFound {
          oracle: oracle,
          identity: validation_oracle_source_identity(source: source),
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

data validation_oracle_pin_scope_note: String = "Not every declared validation names an oracle this pin can cover, and the ones that do not REFUSE rather than verifying unpinned. A gunbc claim validation names an exact entry file and function, so its oracle is a modeled artifact with an observable identity. A cargo test or a rustfmt check does not: the first names a filter over a Rust test corpus whose extent is decided by cargo at run time, the second names a tool whose oracle is its own formatting rules. Both have real oracles; neither has one this module can observe today. Verifying them anyway would hand back a green whose meaning is 'the agent's own edits passed the agent's own tests', which is precisely the fail-open this lane exists to close, so the honest answer is a typed, counted refusal that names the validation that could not be pinned. dissolve-on: feature:validation-oracle-nondag-subjects — a cargo test target and a formatter's rule set each carry an observable identity, at which point these become OraclePinnable rows and this arm loses its producers."

type OraclePinScope
  = OraclePinnable { oracle: ValidationOracleRef }
  | OracleNotPinnable {
      validation_label: String,
      reason: String,
    }

fn validation_oracle_scope(validation: gunbc.roadmap_execution_contract.ValidationOperation) -> OraclePinScope {
  match validation {
    GunbcClaimValidation { entry, function } =>
      OraclePinnable {
        oracle: ValidationOracleRef {
          entry: entry,
          function: function,
        },
      }
    CargoTestValidation { filter: f } =>
      OracleNotPinnable {
        validation_label: join(["cargo-test ", f as String], ""),
        reason: "a cargo test filter names no single observable oracle artifact; its extent is decided by cargo at run time",
      }
    CargoFmtValidation =>
      OracleNotPinnable {
        validation_label: "cargo-fmt",
        reason: "a formatter's oracle is its own rule set, which carries no identity this module can observe",
      }
  }
}

fn validation_oracle_scopes(
  contract: gunbc.roadmap_execution_contract.WorkItemExecutionContract,
) -> List<OraclePinScope> {
  match contract {
    ExecutionContractUnspecified => []
    ExecutionContract { validations, extra_capabilities: _ } =>
      map(validations, v => validation_oracle_scope(validation: v))
  }
}

// A contract may declare several validations, so the pinned fact is one identity over the ORDERED set of their oracles, folded left with content_hash_combine_structural under a tag. One digest rather than a list keeps the persisted pin a single value with a single home, and ordering is the contract's own declaration order, which is already the order the validations run in — so the fold introduces no ordering decision of its own. Any oracle moving moves the combined identity, which is the property the pin needs; WHICH one moved is recovered from the per-oracle observations carried beside the refusal, not from the digest.
type OracleSetObservation
  = OracleSetObserved {
      identity: std.content_hash.ContentHash,
      observations: List<OracleObservation>,
    }
  | OracleSetIncomplete {
      blocking: OracleObservation,
      observations: List<OracleObservation>,
    }

fn validation_oracle_observation_identity(
  observation: OracleObservation,
) -> std.content_hash.Fnv1a64Structural? {
  match observation {
    OracleFound { oracle: _, identity } => Present { value: identity }
    OracleMissing { oracle: _, detail: _ } => none
    OracleAmbiguous { oracle: _, declaration_count: _ } => none
    OracleDependsOnUnpinnedOracle { oracle: _, imports: _ } => none
  }
}

fn validation_oracle_observation_ref(
  observation: OracleObservation,
) -> ValidationOracleRef {
  match observation {
    OracleFound { oracle, identity: _ } => oracle
    OracleMissing { oracle, detail: _ } => oracle
    OracleAmbiguous { oracle, declaration_count: _ } => oracle
    OracleDependsOnUnpinnedOracle { oracle, imports: _ } => oracle
  }
}

fn validation_oracle_refs(
  observations: List<OracleObservation>,
) -> List<ValidationOracleRef> {
  map(observations, o => validation_oracle_observation_ref(observation: o))
}

fn validation_oracle_first_incomplete(
  observations: List<OracleObservation>,
) -> OracleObservation? {
  observations
    |> filter(o => match validation_oracle_observation_identity(observation: o) {
      Present { value: _ } => false
      Absent => true
    })
    |> first
}

fn validation_oracle_set_observe(
  observations: List<OracleObservation>,
) -> OracleSetObservation {
  match validation_oracle_first_incomplete(observations: observations) {
    Present { value: blocking } =>
      OracleSetIncomplete {
        blocking: blocking,
        observations: observations,
      }
    Absent =>
      OracleSetObserved {
        identity: as_content_hash_structural(
          structural: fold(
            observations,
            init: content_hash_atom(value: validation_oracle_set_tag),
            f: fn(acc, o) {
              match validation_oracle_observation_identity(observation: o) {
                Present { value: structural } =>
                  content_hash_combine_structural(left: acc, right: structural)
                Absent => acc
              }
            },
          ),
        ),
        observations: observations,
      }
  }
}

data validation_verdict_note: String = "The typed verdict stored beside the attempt and projected into the Verify lamp. Passed and Failed are the two ANSWERS a run can produce — the claim runner exits nonzero when its Bool entry returns false, so a red witness is data this module adjudicates, not a fault. Every other outcome is a REFUSAL that names its own cause, and no arm widens: there is no arm that runs the validation without a satisfied pin, no arm that treats an unobservable oracle as a pass, and no arm that treats a refusal as a red. The distinction between a refusal and a red is the whole point of the second oracle control — a deleted witness must not read as a failing witness, because the remedies are opposite: one restores the oracle, the other fixes the subject."

type ValidationRefusalCause
  = ValidationContractUnspecified
  | ValidationOracleUnpinnable {
      validation_label: String,
      reason: String,
    }
  | ValidationOracleMissing {
      entry: String,
      function: String,
      detail: String,
    }
  | ValidationOracleAmbiguous {
      entry: String,
      function: String,
      declaration_count: Int,
    }
  | ValidationOracleUnpinnedDependency {
      entry: String,
      imports: List<String>,
    }
  | ValidationOracleMoved {
      expected: std.content_hash.ContentHash,
      observed: std.content_hash.ContentHash,
    }
  | ValidationOracleCrossFamilyIdentityIncomparable {
      expected: std.content_hash.ContentHash,
      observed: std.content_hash.ContentHash,
    }
  | ValidationOraclePinUnrecorded { channel: std.types.NonEmptyStr }
  | ValidationPinReceiptUnreadable { detail: String }
  | ValidationCommandUnrealized
  | ValidationCommandRefused {
      program: String,
      reason: String,
    }
  | ValidationChangeWindowRefused {
      cause: gunbc.git_diff_change_window.ChangeWindowPreparationRefusal,
    }

data validation_not_run_note: String = "review 45271. ValidationNotRun exists so that the ABSENCE of a receipt stops being a state the verification seam has to interpret. The seam previously asked `is the receipt text empty` and got `\"\"` from three different worlds — no file, an unreadable file, and an empty one — then RAN THE VALIDATION AND OVERWROTE whatever was there. Absent and unreadable have opposite correct actions (run it; stop and do not touch the evidence), so collapsing them let an I/O fault regenerate a verdict against a worktree the agent may have moved since. The fix is construction rather than a better classifier, because the Filesystem.Read surface exposes no typed absence and deciding it from an error string would be a guess: dispatch WRITES this verdict beside the attempt during state initialization, exactly as it already writes an empty provider-events.jsonl so that stream's absence is meaningful. After that, a receipt is always present, so a failed read is unambiguously a fault and refuses. The Verify lamp reads this as pending — which makes its pending state evidence-backed rather than inferred from nothing."

type ValidationVerdict
  = ValidationNotRun
  | ValidationPassed {
      command: String,
      oracle_identity: std.content_hash.ContentHash,
    }
  | ValidationFailed {
      command: String,
      exit_code: Int,
      detail: String,
    }
  | ValidationRefused { cause: ValidationRefusalCause }

fn validation_oracle_incomplete_cause(
  observation: OracleObservation,
) -> ValidationRefusalCause {
  match observation {
    OracleFound { oracle: _, identity: _ } =>
      ValidationPinReceiptUnreadable {
        detail: "a found oracle is not an incomplete observation; this arm is unreachable by construction of validation_oracle_set_observe",
      }
    OracleMissing { oracle, detail } =>
      ValidationOracleMissing {
        entry: oracle.entry as String,
        function: oracle.function as String,
        detail: detail,
      }
    OracleAmbiguous { oracle, declaration_count } =>
      ValidationOracleAmbiguous {
        entry: oracle.entry as String,
        function: oracle.function as String,
        declaration_count: declaration_count,
      }
    OracleDependsOnUnpinnedOracle { oracle, imports } =>
      ValidationOracleUnpinnedDependency {
        entry: oracle.entry as String,
        imports: imports,
      }
  }
}

// The pin adjudication is extdeps.pin's admit_pin_integrity and nothing else — this module builds the Pin and reads its answer, so expected-versus-observed has one home. Only expected_identity crosses the disk seam between dispatch and verification, because it is the only fact that cannot be re-derived at verification time: the subject comes from the node's own execution contract, the selection is ExactPin by construction (a dispatch pin records an identity OBSERVED from the freshly created worktree, never one resolved from a moving reference), and the version is the revision that worktree was created at, which is captured beside the identity for the same reason. Re-deriving the subject rather than persisting it is deliberate and it fails closed: if the contract changed between dispatch and verification, the re-derived oracle no longer matches the stored identity and the run refuses, which is the correct answer — the pin recorded at dispatch is no longer the pin the contract now names.
fn validation_oracle_parse_pinned_identity_wire(text: String) -> std.content_hash.ContentHash? {
  if text == "" {
    none
  } else {
    match fnv1a64_structural_hex_digest(hex: text) {
      Present { value: structural } =>
        Present { value: as_content_hash_structural(structural: structural) }
      Absent => none
    }
  }
}

fn validation_oracle_parse_pinned_revision_wire(text: String) -> extdeps.version.VersionIdentity? {
  if text == "" {
    none
  } else {
    Present { value: text as std.types.NonEmptyStr as extdeps.version.VersionIdentity }
  }
}

fn validation_oracle_pin_recorded(
  identity: std.content_hash.ContentHash?,
  revision: extdeps.version.VersionIdentity?,
) -> Bool {
  match identity {
    Present { value: _ } =>
      match revision {
        Present { value: _ } => true
        Absent => false
      }
    Absent => false
  }
}

fn validation_oracle_pin(
  oracles: List<ValidationOracleRef>,
  expected_identity: std.content_hash.ContentHash,
  revision: extdeps.version.VersionIdentity,
) -> Pin<List<ValidationOracleRef>> {
  extdeps.pin.Pin {
    subject: oracles,
    version: revision,
    expected_identity: expected_identity,
    selection: ExactPin,
  }
}

fn validation_oracle_pin_refusal_cause(
  cause: extdeps.pin.PinRefusalCause,
) -> ValidationRefusalCause {
  match cause {
    ObservedIdentityMismatch { expected, observed } =>
      ValidationOracleMoved {
        expected: expected,
        observed: observed,
      }
    TrackedResolutionUnrecorded { channel } =>
      ValidationOraclePinUnrecorded { channel: channel }
    ConstraintIsNotIdentity { constraint } =>
      ValidationPinReceiptUnreadable {
        detail: join([
          "pinned oracle identity is a constraint, not an identity: ",
          constraint as String,
        ], ""),
      }
    PinAbsent =>
      ValidationPinReceiptUnreadable {
        detail: "no oracle identity was recorded for this attempt at dispatch",
      }
    CrossFamilyIdentityIncomparable { expected, observed } =>
      ValidationOracleCrossFamilyIdentityIncomparable {
        expected: expected,
        observed: observed,
      }
    CitedDigestIdentityUnprojectable =>
      ValidationPinReceiptUnreadable {
        detail: "the pin subject's cited digest cannot be projected to ContentHash; the hash bridge refused",
      }
  }
}

type ValidationOracleGate
  = ValidationOracleAdmitted { identity: std.content_hash.ContentHash }
  | ValidationOracleGateRefused { cause: ValidationRefusalCause }

data validation_oracle_gate_note: String = "The one place a validation run becomes permitted. It is a GATE rather than a check beside the runner because the run is an effect: the only way to reach the exec is through an Admitted answer carrying the identity that admitted it, so there is no code path that runs the declared command with an unsatisfied pin. That is weaker than making the bad state unwritable in the type system and it is not claimed to be more — a caller could ignore this fn — but it removes the shape where a run happens first and its pin is consulted afterwards, which is the shape that greens an agent's edits to its own witness."

fn validation_oracle_gate(
  contract: gunbc.roadmap_execution_contract.WorkItemExecutionContract,
  observations: List<OracleObservation>,
  pin_recorded: Bool,
  expected_identity: std.content_hash.ContentHash,
  revision: extdeps.version.VersionIdentity,
  pin_read_detail: String,
) -> ValidationOracleGate {
  match contract {
    ExecutionContractUnspecified =>
      ValidationOracleGateRefused { cause: ValidationContractUnspecified }
    ExecutionContract { validations: _, extra_capabilities: _ } =>
      match validation_oracle_scopes(contract: contract)
        |> filter(s => match s {
          OraclePinnable { oracle: _ } => false
          OracleNotPinnable { validation_label: _, reason: _ } => true
        })
        |> first {
        Present { value: unpinnable } =>
          match unpinnable {
            OracleNotPinnable { validation_label, reason } =>
              ValidationOracleGateRefused {
                cause: ValidationOracleUnpinnable {
                  validation_label: validation_label,
                  reason: reason,
                },
              }
            OraclePinnable { oracle: _ } =>
              ValidationOracleGateRefused {
                cause: ValidationPinReceiptUnreadable {
                  detail: "a pinnable scope is not an unpinnable one; this arm is unreachable by construction of the filter above",
                },
              }
          }
        Absent =>
          match validation_oracle_set_observe(observations: observations) {
            OracleSetIncomplete { blocking, observations: _ } =>
              ValidationOracleGateRefused {
                cause: validation_oracle_incomplete_cause(
                  observation: blocking,
                ),
              }
            OracleSetObserved { identity, observations: _ } =>
              if !pin_recorded {
                ValidationOracleGateRefused {
                  cause: ValidationPinReceiptUnreadable {
                    detail: pin_read_detail,
                  },
                }
              } else {
                match admit_pin_integrity(
                  pin: validation_oracle_pin(
                    oracles: validation_oracle_refs(
                      observations: observations,
                    ),
                    expected_identity: expected_identity,
                    revision: revision,
                  ),
                  observed_identity: identity,
                ) {
                  PinIntegrityAdmitted { pin: _ } =>
                    ValidationOracleAdmitted { identity: identity }
                  PinRefused { subject: _, cause } =>
                    ValidationOracleGateRefused {
                      cause: validation_oracle_pin_refusal_cause(cause: cause),
                    }
                }
            }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn change_window_preparation_refusal_detail(cause: gunbc.git_diff_change_window.ChangeWindowPreparationRefusal) -> String {
  match cause {
    ChangeWindowMergeBaseRefused { observation: observation } =>
      match observation {
        GitMergeBaseProgramUnavailable { program, reason } =>
          join([program, " was not available to observe merge-base: ", reason], "")
        GitMergeBaseExecutionFailed { program, exit_code, stdout, stderr } =>
          join([
            program,
            " merge-base observation failed (exit ",
            to_string(exit_code),
            "): ",
            stderr,
          ], "")
        GitMergeBaseOutputMalformed { stdout } =>
          join(["merge-base observation returned malformed stdout: ", stdout], "")
        GitMergeBaseObserved { target: _, target_generation: _, base: _, head: _ } =>
          "unreachable merge-base observed arm in refusal detail"
      }
    ChangeWindowTargetGenerationRefused { observation: observation } =>
      match observation {
        GitTargetGenerationProgramUnavailable { program, reason } =>
          join([program, " was not available to read target generation: ", reason], "")
        GitTargetGenerationExecutionFailed { program, exit_code, stdout, stderr } =>
          join([
            program,
            " target-generation observation failed (exit ",
            to_string(exit_code),
            "): ",
            stderr,
          ], "")
        GitTargetGenerationOutputMalformed { stdout } =>
          join(["target-generation observation returned malformed stdout: ", stdout], "")
        GitTargetGenerationObserved { generation: _ } =>
          "unreachable target-generation observed arm in refusal detail"
      }
    ChangeWindowHeadObjectIdRefused { head_hex } =>
      join(["verification subject head is not a valid git object id: ", head_hex], "")
    ChangeWindowPersistenceRefused { path, cause } =>
      join(["git diff window receipt could not be written at ", concat(path, ": "), cause], "")
  }
}

fn validation_refusal_cause_key(cause: ValidationRefusalCause) -> String {
  match cause {
    ValidationContractUnspecified => "contract-unspecified"
    ValidationOracleUnpinnable { validation_label: _, reason: _ } =>
      "oracle-unpinnable"
    ValidationOracleMissing { entry: _, function: _, detail: _ } =>
      "oracle-missing"
    ValidationOracleAmbiguous { entry: _, function: _, declaration_count: _ } =>
      "oracle-ambiguous"
    ValidationOracleUnpinnedDependency { entry: _, imports: _ } =>
      "oracle-unpinned-dependency"
    ValidationOracleMoved { expected: _, observed: _ } => "oracle-moved"
    ValidationOracleCrossFamilyIdentityIncomparable { expected: _, observed: _ } =>
      "oracle-cross-family-incomparable"
    ValidationOraclePinUnrecorded { channel: _ } => "oracle-pin-unrecorded"
    ValidationPinReceiptUnreadable { detail: _ } => "pin-receipt-unreadable"
    ValidationCommandUnrealized => "command-unrealized"
    ValidationCommandRefused { program: _, reason: _ } => "command-refused"
    ValidationChangeWindowRefused { cause: _ } => "change-window-refused"
  }
}

fn validation_refusal_cause_detail(cause: ValidationRefusalCause) -> String {
  match cause {
    ValidationContractUnspecified =>
      "the node declares no execution contract, so there is no validation to run"
    ValidationOracleUnpinnable { validation_label, reason } =>
      join([
        "declared validation ",
        validation_label,
        " cannot be pinned: ",
        reason,
      ], "")
    ValidationOracleMissing { entry: _, function: _, detail } => detail
    ValidationOracleAmbiguous { entry, function, declaration_count } =>
      join([
        entry,
        " declares ",
        to_string(declaration_count),
        " declarations named ",
        function,
        "; the contract does not determine which one the claim runner selects",
      ], "")
    ValidationOracleUnpinnedDependency { entry, imports } =>
      join([
        entry,
        " imports ",
        to_string(count(imports)),
        " test-side module(s) whose content this pin does not cover (",
        join(imports, ", "),
        "). Verifying would adjudicate a partially pinned oracle, so the run refuses instead",
      ], "")
    ValidationOracleMoved { expected, observed } =>
      join([
        "the validation oracle changed after dispatch: pinned ",
        expected as String,
        ", observed ",
        observed as String,
        ". A run against an oracle the attempt itself moved proves nothing",
      ], "")
    ValidationOracleCrossFamilyIdentityIncomparable { expected, observed } =>
      join([
        "pinned oracle identity and observed identity are different hash families and cannot be compared: expected ",
        expected as String,
        ", observed ",
        observed as String,
        ". This is a modeling error upstream of the pin gate, not oracle drift",
      ], "")
    ValidationOraclePinUnrecorded { channel } =>
      join([
        "the oracle identity was resolved from the moving reference ",
        channel as String,
        " and no record of that resolution is carried",
      ], "")
    ValidationPinReceiptUnreadable { detail } => detail
    ValidationCommandUnrealized =>
      "the declared validation has no host capability realization, so no command exists to run"
    ValidationCommandRefused { program, reason } =>
      join([program, ": ", reason], "")
    ValidationChangeWindowRefused { cause } =>
      change_window_preparation_refusal_detail(cause: cause)
  }
}

fn validation_refusal_cause_decode(
  cause_kind: String,
  cause_detail: String,
) -> ValidationRefusalCause? {
  if cause_kind == "contract-unspecified" {
    Present { value: ValidationContractUnspecified }
  } else if cause_kind == "command-unrealized" {
    Present { value: ValidationCommandUnrealized }
  } else if cause_kind == "pin-receipt-unreadable" {
    Present {
      value: ValidationPinReceiptUnreadable { detail: cause_detail },
    }
  } else if cause_kind == "command-refused" {
    Present {
      value: ValidationCommandRefused {
        program: "unknown",
        reason: cause_detail,
      },
    }
  } else if cause_kind == "change-window-refused" {
    Present {
      value: ValidationChangeWindowRefused {
        cause: gunbc.git_diff_change_window.ChangeWindowPersistenceRefused {
          path: "",
          cause: cause_detail,
        },
      },
    }
  } else if cause_kind == "oracle-unpinnable" {
    Present {
      value: ValidationOracleUnpinnable {
        validation_label: "unknown",
        reason: cause_detail,
      },
    }
  } else if cause_kind == "oracle-missing" {
    Present {
      value: ValidationOracleMissing {
        entry: "unknown",
        function: "unknown",
        detail: cause_detail,
      },
    }
  } else if cause_kind == "oracle-ambiguous" {
    Present {
      value: ValidationOracleAmbiguous {
        entry: "unknown",
        function: "unknown",
        declaration_count: 0,
      },
    }
  } else if cause_kind == "oracle-unpinned-dependency" {
    Present {
      value: ValidationOracleUnpinnedDependency {
        entry: "unknown",
        imports: [],
      },
    }
  } else if cause_kind == "oracle-pin-unrecorded" {
    Present {
      value: ValidationOraclePinUnrecorded {
        channel: cause_detail as std.types.NonEmptyStr as extdeps.version.VersionIdentity,
      },
    }
  } else {
    none
  }
}

fn validation_verdict_key(verdict: ValidationVerdict) -> String {
  match verdict {
    ValidationNotRun => "not_run"
    ValidationPassed { command: _, oracle_identity: _ } => "passed"
    ValidationFailed { command: _, exit_code: _, detail: _ } => "failed"
    ValidationRefused { cause: _ } => "refused"
  }
}

fn validation_verdict_detail(verdict: ValidationVerdict) -> String {
  match verdict {
    ValidationNotRun =>
      "the declared validation has not run for this attempt; this receipt was written at dispatch so that a later unreadable receipt is a fault rather than an absence"
    ValidationPassed { command, oracle_identity } =>
      join([
        "declared validation passed against the pinned oracle ",
        oracle_identity as String,
        ": ",
        command,
      ], "")
    ValidationFailed { command, exit_code, detail } =>
      join([
        "declared validation returned false (exit ",
        to_string(exit_code),
        ") against the pinned oracle: ",
        command,
        if detail == "" { "" } else { join([" — ", detail], "") },
      ], "")
    ValidationRefused { cause } =>
      join([
        "validation refused (",
        validation_refusal_cause_key(cause: cause),
        "): ",
        validation_refusal_cause_detail(cause: cause),
      ], "")
  }
}

data validation_receipt_schema: std.types.NonEmptyStr = "roadmap-validation-receipt/v1"

data validation_receipt_identity_note: String = "The persisted verdict used to be identified by its LEADING BYTES: the emitter always writes schema then verdict first, so a reader required the document to START with the byte prefix this same emitter derives for a given verdict. That defeated the attack it was built for - a reader that SCANS for the word `passed` is satisfied from any value position, so a refusal whose detail carries the word reads as a pass, and no nested value can occupy a document prefix. It was honest about its ceiling and it has now been reached: a prefix recovers only LEADING members, so every field after the second is unreadable, which is why the receipt could be identified but never actually read. The reader below parses the document into a JsonValue and reads schema and verdict as MEMBERS. That subsumes the positional guarantee rather than weakening it - a `passed` sitting in a detail string is a different member and cannot be mistaken for the verdict, because the reader asks for the verdict by name instead of asking where the bytes fall - and it lifts the ceiling, so the exact-head subject fields the Verify rework adds become readable by the same mechanism with no new decoder."

// Why the identity members are read with json_object_unique_member rather than a find-first: RFC 8259 permits duplicate names and does not say which wins, so a document carrying two verdict members is well-formed JSON with no committed answer. A reader that takes the first or the last silently picks one, and last-wins in particular means appending a second verdict to a refusal turns it into a pass. Duplication is therefore refused here, not resolved, and the same rule extends unchanged to head_sha, attempt_key and node_identity when Verify adds them - which is the point of putting the exactly-once rule in this decoder rather than in the generic parser.
data validation_receipt_verdict_keys: List<String> = ["not_run", "passed", "failed", "refused"]

type ValidationReceiptMember
  = ValidationReceiptMemberRead { value: String }
  | ValidationReceiptMemberUnreadable { reason: String }

fn validation_receipt_string_member(
  doc: extdeps.languages.json.emit.JsonValue,
  key: String,
) -> ValidationReceiptMember {
  match json_object_unique_member(v: doc, key: key) {
    JsonMemberNotAnObject =>
      ValidationReceiptMemberUnreadable {
        reason: "validation receipt is not a JSON object",
      }
    JsonMemberAbsent =>
      ValidationReceiptMemberUnreadable {
        reason: concat("validation receipt has no ", concat(key, " member")),
      }
    JsonMemberDuplicated { count: n } =>
      ValidationReceiptMemberUnreadable {
        reason: concat(
          "validation receipt carries ",
          concat(
            to_string(n),
            concat(
              " ",
              concat(key, " members; an identity member must occur exactly once"),
            ),
          ),
        ),
      }
    JsonMemberFound { value: v } =>
      match v {
        extdeps.languages.json.emit.JsonString { value: sv } => ValidationReceiptMemberRead { value: sv }
        _ =>
          ValidationReceiptMemberUnreadable {
            reason: concat(
              "validation receipt ",
              concat(key, " is not a JSON string"),
            ),
          }
      }
  }
}

fn validation_oracle_observation_json(
  observation: OracleObservation,
) -> extdeps.languages.json.emit.JsonValue {
  match observation {
    OracleFound { oracle, identity } =>
      json_object(members: [
        json_kv(key: "entry", value: json_string(s: oracle.entry as String)),
        json_kv(key: "function", value: json_string(s: oracle.function as String)),
        json_kv(key: "state", value: json_string(s: "found")),
        json_kv(
          key: "identity",
          value: json_string(
            s: serialize_content_hash(
              hash: as_content_hash_structural(structural: identity),
            ),
          ),
        ),
      ])
    OracleMissing { oracle, detail } =>
      json_object(members: [
        json_kv(key: "entry", value: json_string(s: oracle.entry as String)),
        json_kv(key: "function", value: json_string(s: oracle.function as String)),
        json_kv(key: "state", value: json_string(s: "missing")),
        json_kv(key: "detail", value: json_string(s: detail)),
      ])
    OracleDependsOnUnpinnedOracle { oracle, imports } =>
      json_object(members: [
        json_kv(key: "entry", value: json_string(s: oracle.entry as String)),
        json_kv(key: "function", value: json_string(s: oracle.function as String)),
        json_kv(key: "state", value: json_string(s: "unpinned-dependency")),
        json_kv(key: "imports", value: json_array(elements: map(
          imports, m => json_string(s: m),
        ))),
      ])
    OracleAmbiguous { oracle, declaration_count } =>
      json_object(members: [
        json_kv(key: "entry", value: json_string(s: oracle.entry as String)),
        json_kv(key: "function", value: json_string(s: oracle.function as String)),
        json_kv(key: "state", value: json_string(s: "ambiguous")),
        json_kv(key: "declaration_count", value: json_int(n: declaration_count)),
      ])
  }
}

fn validation_receipt_json(
  verdict: ValidationVerdict,
  observations: List<OracleObservation>,
) -> String {
  serialize_json(v: json_object(members: [
    json_kv(key: "schema", value: json_string(s: validation_receipt_schema as String)),
    json_kv(
      key: "verdict",
      value: json_string(s: validation_verdict_key(verdict: verdict)),
    ),
    json_kv(
      key: "detail",
      value: json_string(s: validation_verdict_detail(verdict: verdict)),
    ),
    json_kv(
      key: "oracles",
      value: json_array(elements: map(
        observations,
        o => validation_oracle_observation_json(observation: o),
      )),
    ),
  ]))
}

type ValidationReceiptDecode
  = ValidationReceiptDecoded { verdict_key: String }
  | ValidationReceiptUndecodable { reason: String }

fn validation_receipt_decode_document(doc: extdeps.languages.json.emit.JsonValue) -> ValidationReceiptDecode {
  match validation_receipt_string_member(doc: doc, key: "schema") {
    ValidationReceiptMemberUnreadable { reason: r } =>
      ValidationReceiptUndecodable { reason: r }
    ValidationReceiptMemberRead { value: schema } =>
      if schema != (validation_receipt_schema as String) {
        ValidationReceiptUndecodable {
          reason: concat(
            "validation receipt schema is ",
            concat(
              schema,
              concat(", not ", validation_receipt_schema as String),
            ),
          ),
        }
      } else {
        match validation_receipt_string_member(doc: doc, key: "verdict") {
          ValidationReceiptMemberUnreadable { reason: r } =>
            ValidationReceiptUndecodable { reason: r }
          ValidationReceiptMemberRead { value: verdict } =>
            if count(filter(validation_receipt_verdict_keys, k => k == verdict)) > 0 {
              ValidationReceiptDecoded { verdict_key: verdict }
            } else {
              ValidationReceiptUndecodable {
                reason: concat(
                  "validation receipt verdict ",
                  concat(verdict, " is not one this emitter writes"),
                ),
              }
            }
        }
      }
  }
}

fn validation_receipt_decode(receipt: String) -> ValidationReceiptDecode {
  match parse_json(s: receipt) {
    Absent =>
      ValidationReceiptUndecodable {
        reason: "validation receipt is not one complete JSON value",
      }
    Present { value: doc } => validation_receipt_decode_document(doc: doc)
  }
}
